Package: remotedesktop-host
Version: @VERSION@
Section: net
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCHITECTURE@
Maintainer: Zeqian GAO <zeqian_gao@outlook.com>
Homepage: https://github.com/gaozeqian/RemoteDesktop
Depends: ca-certificates, debconf (>= 0.5) | debconf-2.0, libavcodec62, libavformat62, libavutil60, libc6, libei1, libgcc-s1, libicu78 | libicu77 | libicu76 | libicu74 | libicu72, libpipewire-0.3-0t64 | libpipewire-0.3-0, libpulse0, libssl3t64 | libssl3, libstdc++6, libswscale9, zlib1g
Recommends: xdg-desktop-portal-gnome | xdg-desktop-portal-kde | xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
Description: Remote Desktop host (lets this machine be controlled)
 Lets somebody at another computer see this machine's screen and control its
 keyboard and pointer, after this machine's owner agrees.
 .
 The installation asks for the signaling server's address and, when that
 address is wss:// and the server uses a self-signed certificate, for the
 certificate to trust. No certificate is needed for a ws:// address or for one
 signed by a public authority.
 .
 It runs as a user service inside the graphical session, because that is the
 only place a Wayland desktop will let it in. Screen sharing and control are
 asked for through the desktop's own portal, so the answer is a dialog your
 desktop shows rather than a permission granted in advance.
 .
 Sharing a screen is remembered once you agree to it; control is asked for
 every time. That is the portal's decision rather than this package's — a
 remote-desktop session cannot be persisted — and it means an unattended
 machine can be watched but not typed into without somebody at the keyboard.
 .
 The libraries above are loaded by name at run time, so nothing in the package
 metadata would notice them missing: the failure is a DllNotFoundException the
 first time a screen is captured. libavcodec62 is the one that matters most —
 it is the encoder, and a system where it was built without libx264 falls back
 to whatever else is available or refuses to stream, saying so in the log.
